# Requirements file skeletons

Three files hold the business requirements: one `{{vars.requirementsFile}}` per feature folder, the
index `README.md` and the shared `GLOSSARY.md` at the root of `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/`. Copy the
skeletons when a file is missing; never invent other sections.

## Feature file: `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/<feature>/{{vars.requirementsFile}}`

````markdown
# <Feature name>

<Purpose: 2 to 4 timeless sentences: who uses the capability and what it lets them achieve.>

**Coverage:** complete | partial. **Related rules:** <IDs of other features' rules this one relies on | none>

## Rules

### <PREFIX>-1 <Rule statement>

```gherkin
Rule: <PREFIX>-1 <Rule statement>
  Scenario: <PREFIX>-1.1 <outcome>
    Given <situation>
    When <event>
    Then <observable outcome>
```

## Glossary

| Term | Kind | Meaning | Avoid |
|---|---|---|---|

## Retired IDs

- <ID> <former name>: replaced by <ID> | removed
````

| Part | Rule |
|---|---|
| H1 | The feature's name: the folder's kebab-case noun in words ("Checkout"). |
| Purpose | Timeless and request-free: no "will", "new", ticket keys or requester names. |
| Coverage | `complete` when every known behavior of the feature is described; `partial` otherwise (usual after an adoption baseline). The index row says the same. |
| Related rules | IDs only, never copied text; `none` when there are none. |
| Rules | One H3 and one fenced `gherkin` block per Rule, ordered by ID. The H3 repeats the Rule line without the keyword. A new rule gets the highest rule number ever used in the file plus 1, retired ones included; a new scenario gets the highest number ever used in its rule plus 1. |
| Glossary | Every domain term the file uses, alphabetical. Kind: subject (an actor), object (a thing), event (something that happens), state (a condition), value (an amount, date or quantity). Avoid: synonyms not to use. A term with two meanings gets a qualifier ("Billing Account", "Login Account"). |
| Shared term | Meaning copied word for word from `GLOSSARY.md`, followed by `Shared: [GLOSSARY.md](../GLOSSARY.md)`. |
| Retired IDs | Added with the first retirement; one line per retired rule (its scenarios retire with it) or scenario; lines are never deleted. |

## Index: `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/README.md`

```markdown
# Requirements

Business requirements of {{project.name}}, one folder per feature. The rules in each feature's
{{vars.requirementsFile}} are the source of truth for its behavior. Terms used by several features
are defined in [GLOSSARY.md](GLOSSARY.md).

| Feature | Prefix | Purpose | Coverage |
|---|---|---|---|
| [<feature>](<feature>/{{vars.requirementsFile}}) | <PREFIX> | <one line> | <complete or partial> |
```

- One row per feature, sorted by feature name. Purpose is one line; a cross-cutting feature (access
  control, audit, notifications) starts it with "Cross-cutting:".
- A prefix has 2 to 5 capital letters, is unique in the index, is never `SYS` (the tech design
  baseline) and should not equal a ticket project key of the team: IDs would look like ticket keys.

## Shared glossary: `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/GLOSSARY.md`

```markdown
# Glossary

Terms used by more than one feature. Each feature's glossary copies the Meaning of the terms it
uses word for word and links here.

| Term | Kind | Meaning | Avoid | Used by |
|---|---|---|---|---|
```

`Used by` lists the features whose glossaries copy the term: all of them change when its Meaning
changes.

## Filled example: `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/checkout/{{vars.requirementsFile}}`

````markdown
# Checkout

Lets a Customer turn the Items of a Cart into a placed Order. Who may place Orders is decided by
the accounts feature.

**Coverage:** partial. **Related rules:** ACC-2

## Rules

### CHK-1 An Order can be placed only for a Cart with at least one Item

```gherkin
Rule: CHK-1 An Order can be placed only for a Cart with at least one Item
  Scenario: CHK-1.1 A Customer places an Order for a Cart with Items
    Given a Customer has a Cart with 2 Items
    When the Customer places an Order
    Then an Order with the 2 Items is placed
  Scenario: CHK-1.3 A Customer cannot place an Order for an empty Cart
    Given a Customer has an empty Cart
    When the Customer tries to place an Order
    Then no Order is placed
    And the Customer is told that the Cart is empty
```

## Glossary

| Term | Kind | Meaning | Avoid |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cart | object | The Items a Customer has chosen but not yet ordered | Basket |
| Customer | subject | A person who places Orders. Shared: [GLOSSARY.md](../GLOSSARY.md) | Client, User |
| Item | object | Something for sale, in the quantity the Customer chose | Line, Product |
| Order | object | A Customer's binding request to buy the Items of a Cart | Purchase |

## Retired IDs

- CHK-1.2 A Customer places an Order without signing in: removed
- CHK-2 Orders under 10 USD are rejected: removed
````
